\section{The \code{ssd()} term}{

The text is a term of the formula, written \code{ssd()}: \code{score ~ age + ssd()}. A formula without an \code{ssd()} term gets \code{+ ssd()} appended, and
without a formula the model is the intercept plus the text. The term can
interact with covariates: in \code{score ~ age + sex * ssd()} (treatment
coding), \code{ssd()} is the gradient at the reference level of \code{sex}, and
\code{sexM:ssd()} the difference between level \code{M} and the reference, so the
gradient at \code{M} is the sum of the two. \code{score ~ sex:ssd()} has one gradient
per level, and in \code{score ~ age * ssd()} \code{ssd()} is the gradient at \code{age = 0}. Each term is then a unit of the result (\code{res$units}, keyed
\code{"sexM:ssd()"}, ...). A formula with \code{ssd(...)} is used as written, so
\code{~ sex:ssd()} has no main text term. \code{ssd()} cannot appear inside a
random-effect term.

The term is never evaluated: \code{fit_regression()} reads it from the formula
and replaces it with \code{(.ssd_pca_1 + ... + .ssd_pca_K)} before a model
function sees the formula. Names starting with \code{.ssd_} are reserved.
}

\section{Several texts}{

\code{s} can be a named list of texts, each named in the formula as
\code{ssd(name)}: \code{fit_regression(list(essay = s1, diary = s2), score ~ age + ssd(essay) + sex * ssd(diary), data = df)}. A name that is not an element
of \code{s} is built from the column of that name in \code{data} as
\code{ssd(data[[name]])}, with every default and one \code{\link[=load_wordpack]{load_wordpack()}} for all
such texts. When every text comes from a column, the formula can come
first: \code{fit_regression(score ~ ssd(essay), data = df)}. A row stays when it
has a valid outcome and a vector in every text. Each text has its own
standardization, PCA and K (\code{fixed_k} applies to all).
}

\section{Choosing K}{

Unless \code{fixed_k} is given, K is chosen once per text by a sweep over
\code{seq(k_min, k_max, k_step)}: OLS of the standardized outcome on the
standardized components, whatever the family, scoring each K by the
interpretability of the clusters of neighbours of both poles and by the
stability of the gradient, and picking the best joint score. The per-K
table is \code{res$texts[[i]]$sweep}.
}

\section{Two model paths}{

A model without random effects, Gaussian with the identity link, is fitted
with \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}; binomial and Poisson models without random effects with
\code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}}. A formula with random effects (\code{(1 | g)}) or another family
or link is fitted with the fastglmm package, which must be installed. Both
paths give the numbers of Python's ssdiff.

fastglmm takes column names of letters, digits and underscores only,
starting with a letter: a formula column such as \code{age.years} raises on
that path (rename it), and \code{data} columns starting with \code{ssd__} are
reserved. There the text's columns are passed as \code{ssd__pca_1}, ..., so the
coefficient names of \code{res$model} use \code{ssd__} where everything else in the
result shows \code{.ssd_}.
}

\section{Tests}{

\code{res$test} holds a refit test of all text terms (an F test for OLS, else a
likelihood-ratio test; on fastglmm as fastglmm reports it), with several
texts one refit test per text, and with a text that has several terms a
Wald test per term label (\code{sex:ssd()} tests whether the gradient differs by
\code{sex}). \code{res$terms} has a Wald test per unit, and \code{res$model_test} the
whole model against \code{outcome ~ 1}.
}

